Zune68 native compatibility and measurements
==========================================

Scope
-----
The native build targets AmigaOS 3.1+, a 68000 and no FPU. Classic MUI 3.8/3.9
application behavior is the priority. The bundled classes remain separate
libraries. Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui provide the image classes used by
the built-in preferences pages. AROS-only module ports, muiscreen.library
and newer MUI features without an identified application need are deferred.

Version policy
--------------
The two native library names share release 19.81 (Alpha 1). Built-in classes retain
their implementation version 1.1, including classes which inherit Notify's
version queries. These numbers identify this implementation; they do not
promise every feature of a proprietary MUI release with the same number.
External MCCs retain their own version reporting. Do not raise class
versions merely to bypass an application's feature checks.

Native release and built-in version definitions are centralized in
native_version.h, used by the native GCC build. Version 19.81 follows
the retained AROS source baseline; Alpha 1 identifies this native port.

Implemented areas with isolated linked-code checks
-------------------------------------------------
- Public library gates, register hooks and classic structure prefixes
- Built-in and external class ownership, failure cleanup and expunge
- Notification removal, re-entry, source disposal and symbolic destinations
- Typed configuration lookup, numeric defaults and custom data pointers
- Buffered input, ARexx fallback hooks, AppIcon drops, legacy menus and help
- Floattext wrapping, dynamic group relayout and explicit cycle ordering
- Numeric scale intervals, reverse mapping and large-range arithmetic
- Signed String selection ranges and explicit up/down focus targets
- Bitmap remap ownership, image allocation failure paths and nested clips
- Pixmap raw/RLE/bzip2 decoding, data replacement and failed-allocation cleanup

These checks execute m68k code with modelled OS services. They are not an
AmigaOS application compatibility matrix or a physical-hardware result.
Use separate OS installations when comparing MUI and Zune68, particularly
with third-party MCCs which open muimaster.library themselves.

Rendering and lifetime limits
-----------------------------
Planar Bitmap rendering treats nonzero global alpha as opaque while
retaining its mask; zero alpha is transparent. Fractional alpha blending
and the core CyberGraphX path are not provided. Window refresh follows the configured preferences. Both refresh modes
use clip regions outside an update, and managed refresh preserves pending
damage while changing regions. Broad redraw/text caches need measurements
and GUI evidence before adoption.

Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui are separate, demand-loaded libraries. Pixmap
accepts RGB24, ARGB32 and CLUT8 images with raw, RLE or bzip2 storage.
Planar screens use graphics.library bitmaps, acquire only the pens actually
used, and approximate fractional alpha with a 4x4 coverage mask. They do
not require CyberGraphX. Truecolor rendering requires CyberGraphX v41;
its classic read/write API supplies software alpha blending.

Bzip2 scratch space is bounded by the declared uncompressed image size,
using the small-memory decoder. A 24x19 ARGB image needs about 70 KiB at
peak during decoding, rather than a 900 KiB block's full scratch space.
Decoded data is retained for reuse; bitmap, mask and pen ownership follows
the screen setup/cleanup lifetime. Borrowed raw image data remains owned
by the caller. Truecolor rendering and physical hardware need separate
guest coverage beyond the planar Prefs checks.

Notification list delivery tolerates callbacks removing notifications or
disposing a plain Notify source. An arbitrary class method may still need
its own instance after sending a notification. Defer disposal of such
objects through the application's pushed-method queue until the callback
unwinds. Explicit notification destinations remain caller-owned: remove
notifications before destroying independently owned targets.

Diagnostic TRACE=1 builds use a bounded 64 KiB T:zune.log fallback, with
messages truncated to 511 bytes and files closed after each write. Shell
output remains caller-owned. Use TRACE=0 for performance measurements.

TheBar
------
TheBar 26.22, TheBarVirt, TheButton, TheBar.mcp and the Toolbar wrapper are
built for the 68000 without an FPU and loaded only when requested. The five
stripped modules total 184,200 bytes. SDK headers, catalogs, documentation
and Examples/TheBarDemo are included. See Docs/thebar/Native for details.

On the isolated 68020 / AmigaOS 3.2.3 guest, TheBarDemo CHECK completes three
construction, attribute, window-open and disposal cycles. The preferences
page renders and Cancel exits with return code zero. This does not cover
all optional RTG effects or every legacy Toolbar application's behavior.

IBrowse progress and limits
---------------------------
IBrowse 3.0a OS3 starts with the native library. Typed configuration defaults
restore its layout, and custom background callbacks now paint the About
page. The callback flag belongs to each object: changing a containing group
must not disable the document painter. Reference MUI 3.9 probes establish
this behavior, callback bounds and the attribute getter. Virtual groups
also bracket content painting with the private MUI begin/end methods.
IBrowse uses these to prepare its paint rectangle: the About text is now
visible on the initial draw without selecting it. The default begin/end
methods install and remove the outer virtual clip, so subclass clipping
cannot remain installed over the toolbar or location field. Edited URL
text now repaints, and the SASG quick-link starts loading its target.

The native title bar provides Settings and Iconify buttons, using the
existing window-button preferences. Explicitly saved settings are retained.
Iconify/restore and opening application settings have been exercised in the
isolated AmigaOS 3.2.3 guest. Snapshot and popup buttons remain unsupported.

IBrowse is not yet a completed compatibility result. Exposure redraws can
leave stale toolbar pixels; colors and navigation redraws still need
investigation. Timer callback arguments now match the reference, fixing the observed
IBrowse timer write into class memory. URL replacement and About navigation
work in the updated guest. Forms, tabs, downloads, HTTPS and shutdown/memory
measurements remain part of the application test matrix.

Runtime workload
----------------
From an Amiga Shell, after installing and rebooting:

    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 >RAM:zune68-contracts.txt
    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 gui >RAM:zune68-gui.txt

The executable requests a 64 KiB stack. The optional gui mode repeatedly
opens and disposes a window, including group relayout. Run on a test
installation: these workloads exercise code which still needs GUI review.

The output separates deterministic 'contract' rows from 'metric' and
'memory' rows. It records notification values, numeric scaling, sorted
insertion, comparison calls, elapsed DOS ticks, free Chip/Fast RAM and
largest blocks. Numeric cases use observed MUI 3.8/3.9 results for shifted,
reversed and single-value ranges.
Two lifecycle iterations warm caches before the repeated-run baseline.
Library closure does not force expunge; the 'closed' sample may retain
idle classes or other OS resources. No automatic memory threshold is
claimed: other tasks, caches and fragmentation affect the measurements.

Repeat with 10, 100 and 1000 iterations and two simultaneous clients.
Record CPU/clock, OS, screen depth, RAM, compiler flags and exact binaries.
Compare trends, not a single free-memory sample. Existing opentest and
Prefs remain the interactive checks for keyboard focus, layout, clipboard,
MCP pages, Use/Save/Cancel, ARexx and Workbench integration.

Original SDK comparison
-----------------------
On the build host, point make at an extracted original SDK:

    make check-sdk MUI38_SDK=/path/to/MUI/Developer
    make check-sdk MUI_SDK=/path/to/SDK/MUI MUI_SDK_VERSION=3.9

This compiles the same client using the original public definitions and
regenerates only the obsolete GCC inline assembly from that SDK's FD and
prototypes. It leaves the original SDK untouched and does not redistribute
it. The outputs are build/muimaster/sdk-check/compatcheck-mui38 and
compatcheck-mui39, respectively.

Run that binary in separate MUI and Zune68 installations, then compare:

    python3 tools/compare_compat.py mui-contracts.txt zune68-contracts.txt

Matching contract output is one narrow result. Inspect timing/memory
separately and exercise real application/MCC versions before making broad
compatibility claims. Hosted GitHub Actions and physical hardware
still require their own runs.
